  "overview": {
    "asOf": "2026-10-14",
    "items": [
      {
        "text": "Rob Bonta is the California Attorney General, appointed in 2021 and elected to a full term in 2022. Before that, he served in the State Assembly for more than eight years and worked as a deputy city attorney for San Francisco. He also served on the Alameda City Council.",
        "cites": [1, 2, 3],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "Rob Bonta is the California Attorney General, appointed in 2021 and elected to a full term in 2022.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "Before that, he served in the State Assembly for more than eight years and worked as a deputy city attorney for San Francisco.",
            "cites": [2]
          },
          {
            "text": "He also served on the Alameda City Council.",
            "cites": [3]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Since the reelection of Donald Trump, Bonta has filed or joined more than 50 lawsuits against his administration. In 2026, he secured a victory that prevented the president from keeping California National Guard troops under federal control indefinitely. During his tenure, he created an Affordability Response Team within the Department of Justice to investigate practices that unlawfully raise costs for Californians. His office secured a $17 billion proposed settlement with Meta over claims that Instagram and Facebook harmed children’s mental health. He also led a coalition of attorneys general in a settlement with Paramount Skydance over its merger with Warner Bros. Discovery, requiring an additional $1.5 billion in domestic film production over five years and annual film release commitments. His office secured a sentence against a real estate agent who attempted to raise rent by 38% for a couple who lost their home in the Eaton Fire.",
        "cites": [1, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "Since the reelection of Donald Trump, Bonta has filed or joined more than 50 lawsuits against his administration.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "In 2026, he secured a victory that prevented the president from keeping California National Guard troops under federal control indefinitely.",
            "cites": [4]
          },
          {
            "text": "During his tenure, he created an Affordability Response Team within the Department of Justice to investigate practices that unlawfully raise costs for Californians.",
            "cites": [5]
          },
          {
            "text": "His office secured a $17 billion proposed settlement with Meta over claims that Instagram and Facebook harmed children’s mental health.",
            "cites": [6]
          },
          {
            "text": "He also led a coalition of attorneys general in a settlement with Paramount Skydance over its merger with Warner Bros. Discovery, requiring an additional $1.5 billion in domestic film production over five years and annual film release commitments.",
            "cites": [7]
          },
          {
            "text": "His office secured a sentence against a real estate agent who attempted to raise rent by 38% for a couple who lost their home in the Eaton Fire.",
            "cites": [8]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "In the State Assembly, Bonta authored legislation to protect renters from wrongful evictions and wrote the law that made California the first state to ban for-profit prisons. He also authored a law to automatically expunge records for minor marijuana charges following statewide legalization.",
        "cites": [9],
        "sentences": [
